      moduleTitle: 'Stage A — Orientation, Terminal, Git & Computing Foundations',
      title: '0.0A What is a Backend? Servers, Terminal, Files, Processes & Ports',
      summary: 'Absolute beginner starting point: what a backend server actually does, how an OS process listens on a network port, how environment variables (.env) configure programs, and why clients never talk directly to databases.',
      diagram: '[Browser / Mobile App] ---> (IP Address + Port :8080) ---> [Go Server Process (PID)] ---> [Database File / PostgreSQL]',
      whatIsIt: 'A backend is a program running on a computer (server) that waits for network requests on a specific Port, enforces security and business rules, reads or writes a database, and sends back a structured response.',
      howItWorksInternally: 'When you start a compiled Go binary, the Operating System creates a Process with a Process ID (PID), virtual memory space, file descriptors, and an environment variable table (os.Getenv). When the process binds to a TCP port (e.g. :8080), the OS kernel routes packets arriving for that port to your process.',
      whyWeNeedIt: 'If a web page or mobile app connected directly to your database, any user could inspect the app, extract the database password, and delete or steal every customer record.',
      whyPreferred: 'Centralizing rules in a compiled Go server gives you one authoritative boundary for validation, authentication, rate limiting, and database transactions.',
      whenToUse: 'Every web app, mobile app, payment webhook, or multi-user system where data must be shared, validated, and protected.',
      whenNotToUse: 'Purely offline client-side calculators or static brochure pages with no shared state or secrets.',
      advantages: 'Protects database credentials; enforces business invariants; compiles to a single fast native binary in Go.',
      disadvantages: 'Requires running and monitoring a server process and managing network/database configuration.',
      alternatives: 'Static files on a CDN (no dynamic state) or Serverless functions (FaaS, higher cold-start latency).',
      incorrectConsequences: 'Hardcoding ports or database passwords inside source code makes the binary impossible to reconfigure across environments and leaks secrets into Git.',
      realWorldUsage: 'Go Backend Academy itself reads PORT, DB_DRIVER, POSTGRES_PORT, and SQLITE_PATH from .env / environment variables at startup.',
      perfSecMaintScalability: 'Security: Secrets stay in environment variables, never in Git. Maintainability: One binary runs anywhere by changing .env.',
      beginner: 'Think of a restaurant: the dining room menu is the Frontend (what the customer sees), the kitchen is the Backend (where orders are checked and cooked), and the locked vault/pantry is the Database. Customers never walk into the vault—they give their order to the kitchen via a numbered counter window (a Port like 8080).',
      intermediate: 'At the OS level, a backend is a long-running user-space process. It reads runtime settings from environment variables via os.Getenv, opens a listening TCP socket on HOST:PORT, and multiplexes incoming client connections across lightweight goroutines.',
      advanced: 'Under Linux/Windows, listening on a TCP port allocates a socket file descriptor in the kernel table. Twelve-Factor App architecture mandates strict separation of config from code via environment variables so immutable container images can be promoted from staging to prod.',
      incorrectTitle: 'Anti-Pattern: Hardcoding Port and Database Password in Source Code',
      incorrectCode: 'package main\n\n// BUG: Changing port requires recompiling; password is leaked in Git!\nconst ServerPort = "8080"\nconst DatabasePassword = "prod-secret-password"',
      failureAnalysis: 'Anyone with read access to the Git repository now owns your production database password, and deploying on a host where port 8080 is busy fails unless you edit code.',
      correctedTitle: 'Production Pattern: Reading Port from Environment with Safe Default',
      correctedCode: 'package main\n\nimport "os"\n\nfunc ResolvePort(defaultPort string) string {\n\tif v := os.Getenv("PORT"); v != "" {\n\t\treturn v\n\t}\n\treturn defaultPort\n}',
      engineeringReasoning: 'os.Getenv("PORT") allows .env, systemd, Docker, or Kubernetes to inject any port at runtime while falling back to defaultPort in local dev.',
      exTitle: 'Resolve Server Port from Environment with Safe Default',
      exTask: 'Implement ResolvePort(defaultPort string) string that reads os.Getenv("PORT") and returns it if non-empty, otherwise returning defaultPort.',
      exStarter: 'package main\n\nimport "os"\n\nfunc ResolvePort(defaultPort string) string {\n\t// STEP: Read os.Getenv("PORT") and return it if non-empty, else return defaultPort\n\treturn ""\n}',
      exOutput: 'PASS: TestResolvePortFromEnv (0.00s)\nPASS: TestResolvePortFallback (0.00s)',
      exHints: ['Call v := os.Getenv("PORT")', 'If v != "" return v; otherwise return defaultPort.'],
      exTests: ['Verify ResolvePort returns os.Getenv("PORT") when set', 'Verify ResolvePort returns defaultPort when PORT is empty'],
      exTokens: ['os.Getenv("PORT")', 'return defaultPort'],
      exSolution: 'package main\n\nimport "os"\n\nfunc ResolvePort(defaultPort string) string {\n\tif v := os.Getenv("PORT"); v != "" {\n\t\treturn v\n\t}\n\treturn defaultPort\n}',
      exExplanation: 'os.Getenv reads the process environment variable table populated by the OS or .env loader.',
      exChallenge: 'Also read os.Getenv("HOST") and combine host + ":" + port.',
